# ================= HOW TO READ =================
h1('How This Script Is Built')
para("Before we go line by line, this is how the whole thing is laid out. The video is 18 minutes and 50 seconds long, and it has two parts:")
bullets(['An intro that hooks you and makes one big promise: get richer than 99% of people with 30 minutes a day.',
         'Five steps that pay off that promise, one at a time.'], numbered=True)
para("Every step follows the same pattern. First, an open loop, which is a line that makes you curious about what's coming next. Then the why, the what and the how. I'll explain each one as we hit it.")
para("Two more things we decided before writing a single word:")
para("**Who it's for.** Someone who hasn't made their first $1,000 online yet. They know they're broke, and some of them know people make money online, but they've never heard of Jordan's program.")
para("**What it sells.** This video is a mini sales video for $37 program, Digital Launchpad, which Jordan runs with Iman Gadzhi. So every step had to do two jobs: help the viewer for free, and make the program feel like the obvious next move.")
para("Now, you might be thinking: \"Bryan, my video isn't selling a $37 course.\" That's fine. Yours is selling a call, a service or a program. The job is the same. Keep reading.")

# ================= INTRO =================
h1('The Intro, Line By Line')
para("The intro runs from the first line to the start of step 1. In this script it's about 620 words, so roughly 4 minutes. The hook is the first 30 seconds of that, and it's where most videos lose half their viewers. So every single sentence here has a job. If a line doesn't make you want to hear the next one, it gets cut.")
h2('Line 1: The Shocking Question')
para('*"What if I told you that getting richer than 99% of people is actually easier than you think and all it takes is just 30 minutes a day?"*')
para("This line confirms the click. The title says getting rich is easy, and the very first sentence says the same thing back, so the viewer knows they're in the right place.")
para("It's also framed as a question, which makes the viewer's brain want to answer it. And look at \"30 minutes a day\". That answers the first thing a beginner wonders: how much time is this going to cost me?")
para("This is the first rule of hooks I teach: confirm the click in the first 5 seconds. Don't make people wait for a backstory they never asked for.")
h2('Line 2: The Big Promise')
para('*"Well, if you follow the strategy I\'m about to share with you in this video, I promise you will make more progress in one year than most people will in a lifetime."*')
para("This sets the stakes. One year versus a lifetime is a huge gap, and the viewer wants to be on the right side of it.")
para("I split a hook into three parts: the payoff (what you'll get), the context (why it matters) and the proof (why you should believe it). Line 1 was the payoff. This line is the context.")
h2('Line 3: Why Should I Believe You?')
para('*"And look, I started doing this a few years ago when I was completely broke and today even after making over $10 million online this strategy is still something that I follow religiously every single day."*')
para("The viewer is thinking: \"Okay, but how do I know this works?\" This line answers it before they can click away.")
para("And it does it with a before and after. Completely broke, then $10 million. Proof is the fastest way to switch off a viewer's BS detector. And it has to be specific, because people assume you're telling the truth when you give them a real number.")
h2('Line 4: What You\'ll Get')
para('*"So today, I\'m going to share it with you and tell you why it worked so well and show you how you can apply it to your life to get disgustingly rich."*')
para("This line sets expectations. It tells the viewer exactly what's in the video: the strategy, why it works, and how to use it. That's the why, the what and the how, promised up front.")
h2('Lines 5 to 15: The Life You Don\'t Want')
para('*"First, you need to understand how the average person operates and how to break free from this trap before it\'s too late."*')
para("Now the script goes low on purpose. For the next couple of minutes, Jordan paints a picture of the average person. A boring 9 to 5, no goals, money spent on stupid stuff, 4 hours a night scrolling social media.")
para('*"They are literally playing life on autopilot and never take action to improve themselves."*')
para("This is the pain state. It's the life the viewer is scared of ending up in, and some of them are already in it. I call this roller coaster scripting: take the viewer low first, so the high that comes next feels bigger.")
para("Notice the softener too: *\"Now, I'm not judging anyone.\"* Without that line, the section sounds like an attack. With it, Jordan sounds like a friend telling you the truth.")
h2('Line 16: The Turn')
para('*"What I\'m trying to say here is that the bar is so low these days the competition is literally non-existent."*')
para("This is the belief shift. The viewer walked in thinking getting rich is hard. The script has just shown them that most people aren't even trying, so beating them sounds very doable.")
para("I call these belief-breaking points. A viewer won't act on your advice until they believe they can actually do it, so you have to break the old belief before you teach anything.")
h2('Line 17: The Math')
para('*"Now I know 30 minutes might not seem like a lot but just that short amount of time towards something meaningful will turn into 15 hours of focused work in a month and 180 hours in a year."*')
para("Here the script hears the objection coming: \"Only 30 minutes? That sounds too good to be true.\" So it does the math out loud. 30 minutes a day becomes 180 hours a year, which makes the promise feel real.")
para("I call this answering the question in their head. You guess the question in the viewer's head and answer it before they ask.")
h2('Line 18: What You Don\'t Have To Do')
para('*"And to do that, I\'m not telling you to meditate for an hour a day or making you wake up at 5:00 a.m. to go for a run like David Goggins nor am I telling you to work for 12 hours a day for 6 months straight."*')
para("This is the E in NESB, an old copywriting checklist that stands for New, Easy, Safe and Big. A promise sells best when it feels new, easy, safe and big all at once. This line is pure easy. It lists all the painful things the viewer is scared they'll be asked to do, and rules every one of them out.")
para("Then it closes the intro with a promise: *\"if you follow these steps I'm about to share with you you will succeed I promise you.\"*")
para("Count what the intro did before the first step even started. It confirmed the click, set the stakes, gave proof, set expectations, showed the pain, flipped a belief, killed the biggest objection and made it feel easy.")
para("**For your business:** your proof line is a client result or your own before and after with a real number. Your \"what you don't have to do\" line lists the things your buyer dreads, like cold calling, hiring or running ads.")
para("**Try this:** take the intro of your last video and write the job of each sentence next to it. If you can't name the job, cut the sentence.")

# ================= STEP 1 =================
h1('Step 1: Believe You Can Get Rich')
h2('The Open Loop')
para('*"The first step is the most important but gets constantly overlooked by beginners, and this right here is the foundation of all my success today."*')
para("This is an open loop. It doesn't say what the step is yet. It says the step is important and that most people miss it, which makes the viewer want to know if they're missing it too.")
para("I use this exact transition all the time: \"the first step is by far the most important, yet also the most overlooked.\" Every open loop should do three things: point back to what the viewer just got, make them curious, and promise that what's coming next is worth even more.")
h2('The Why')
para('*"Most people never make any bread because they don\'t believe that they can become rich, but I just want to tell you no matter where you come from it\'s possible to get richer than 99% of people."*')
para("This line encourages the viewer's dream. There's an old persuasion line from Blair Warren that says people will do almost anything for someone who encourages their dreams, justifies their failures, calms their fears, confirms their suspicions and helps them throw rocks at their enemies. You'll see all five of those in this script.")
para('*"Look, I grew up with a single mom in massive debt and when I decided to start my own business I went all the way down to $30,000 in debt myself just a few years ago."*')
para("This is the relatability line. The viewer is broke, so Jordan shows he was broke too, with a real number. Once a viewer thinks \"he was just like me\", they start to trust him.")
para("Then comes a story with a source. Earl Nightingale's \"The Strangest Secret\" says that out of 100 people who start even at 25, by 65 only one will be rich. A statistic from a famous book is easier to believe than Jordan saying the same thing himself.")
h2('The What')
para('*"You need to actually brainwash yourself to believe that you can get rich before anything else."*')
para("Here the step finally gets named. And look at where it sits: after the why. The script sold the viewer on why belief matters before telling them what to do about it.")
para("We did that deliberately. If you open a point with the what, the viewer can decide \"I already know that\" and skip ahead. If you open with the why, they don't know what's coming yet, so they stay.")
para('*"Broke is not a condition of the wallet, it\'s a condition of the mind."*')
para("And that's a zinger, a short line people remember and repeat. Every step in a good script needs at least one.")
h2('The How')
para('*"That\'s why for the next 30 days, I challenge you to commit to reading one chapter per day of a book. It will only take you 10 to 15 minutes."*')
para("The how is one action the viewer can start today. One chapter a day, 10 to 15 minutes. It fits inside the 30 minutes promised in the intro, so the promise holds.")
para("Then Jordan names two specific books, As a Man Thinketh and The Millionaire Fastlane, and tells you which one he read right before his first $10,000 online. Naming the books makes it something you can do in the next 24 hours, which is the bar I set for every how.")
h2('The Close')
para('*"I know as I\'m saying this 95% of you won\'t actually sit down and even read one page a day but that\'s fine because it backs up the whole point I\'m trying to make in this video."*')
para("This is a challenge. It dares the viewer to prove him wrong. Nobody wants to be in the 95%, so the line pushes them to act and to keep watching.")
para("It also ties step 1 back to the big idea of the video: the bar is low, and you can be one of the few who clear it. Every step in this script loops back to the title like this.")

para("**For your business:** your step 1 is usually the belief your buyer needs before anything else works. For a sales consultant, it might be \"you don't need more leads, you need to follow up with the ones you have\". Back it with one statistic or one client story.")

# ================= STEP 2 =================
h1('Step 2: Set One 30-Day Goal (And Where The Offer Goes)')
h2('The Open Loop')
para('*"Now once you\'ve built this foundation, step number two involves setting a clear and achievable 30-day goal to put your new knowledge into practice."*')
para("It points back to step 1 (\"this foundation\"), and then moves forward. That's the open loop doing its first job, acknowledging what the viewer just got.")
h2('Breaking A Belief')
para('*"Most people tell you to set 1 year, 5 year, and 10 year goals but I\'m telling you setting big long-term goals is usually discouraging, especially if you\'re at the beginning."*')
para("This goes against common advice, which makes people lean in. Then it softens straight away so it doesn't sound reckless: *\"I'm not saying that you shouldn't dream big.\"*")
h2('Making It Specific')
para('*"Don\'t make your goal something vague like \'I want to start a business and become a millionaire\'. It needs to be a clear and trackable goal like I want to make $1,000 from Dropshipping in the next 30 days."*')
para("This is where the script gets very specific, and the example is chosen carefully. $1,000 from dropshipping is exactly the first win the Digital Launchpad buyer wants. The viewer hears the example and thinks \"that's me\".")
para("Then the how: work backwards from the goal, and write down what skills you need, how much time it takes and what you'll do every day.")
h2('The Offer')
para('*"And if you feel stuck and you\'re not sure which skill you should pick I just launched a new program with my boy Iman Gadzhi called the Digital Launchpad for just $37."*')
para("This is the pitch, and it lands in step 2 of 5. That's early, and it was a deliberate choice.")
para("Look at the line just before it. The script tells the viewer to learn a skill, and then shows them that they don't know which one to pick. Then the offer shows up as the answer to that exact problem. So it lands as help, because it answers the question the viewer has right then.")
para("Why so early? Because most people never reach the end of a video. Check the retention graph on any long video and you'll see a big chunk of viewers gone by the halfway mark. Put your offer at minute 12 and you're pitching to a fraction of the people who clicked. I'd rather pitch to everyone who's still there after the first real payoff.")
para("The rule we follow: deliver one or two real payoffs first, so you've earned some trust, then pitch before people start dropping off. For a video around 15 to 20 minutes long, that usually lands somewhere between minute 3 and minute 7.")
para("**Try this:** open YouTube Studio, click your last video, then Analytics, then Engagement. The audience retention graph shows how many people are still watching at each moment. Find where it drops below 50%. Your offer needs to come before that point.")

# ================= STEP 3 =================
h1('Step 3: Collect Small Wins Every Day')
h2('The Open Loop')
para('*"The third step is one that might seem insignificant but actually has the biggest impact in the long run."*')
para("\"Might seem insignificant\" makes the viewer curious, and \"the biggest impact\" promises that this step is worth even more than the last one.")
h2('The Comment Prompt')
para('*"That\'s why I\'m always saying \'Another day, Another W\'... Go ahead and comment \'another day, another W\' down below."*')
para("This is an engagement call to action. It's quick and easy, and it gives the viewer something to do with their hands. A comment section full of the same phrase also tells new viewers there's a community here.")
h2('Making It Feel Doable')
para('*"A small win is finding your first product scrolling on TikTok, another small win might be building your first store, another one could be making your first TikTok ad. Over the course of a week, you\'d have literally launched a business from scratch."*')
para("This takes a scary goal and cuts it into tiny wins anyone can picture themselves doing. That's what makes it believable. It's also another nudge toward Digital Launchpad, because those three wins are exactly what the program teaches.")
para('*"Again I\'m not telling you to go out and build the next Apple or Tesla."*')
para("That makes it feel easy again. Then the script calls out who it's for: people who are so busy worrying about making millions that they never take the first step toward their first $1,000.")
para("Calling out your viewer like this is how you make the right person feel like the video was made for them, and the wrong person click away. If you sell something, you want both of those to happen.")
para("The step ends with a personal example. On a bad day, Jordan's small win is going to the gym. It makes the advice feel like something he actually does.")

para("**For your business:** cut your client's big goal into wins they could hit this week, like sending 5 follow-ups or rewriting one email. When your viewer can picture doing it, they believe it.")

# ================= STEP 4 =================
h1('Step 4: Avoid The Money Traps')
h2('The Open Loop')
para('*"Now while it\'s important for you to stay disciplined in your life it is equally as important for you to stay disciplined with your finances."*')
para("This step gives the idea a name: money traps. I always tell writers to name their ideas, because people remember a name much more easily than a definition.")
h2('Building Common Ground')
para('*"...buying that expensive crap that you don\'t really need to impress people that you barely like."*')
para("That's a Fight Club reference. A line the viewer already knows from a movie makes them relax, because it feels familiar. I call this building common ground.")
h2('The Damaging Admission')
para('*"When I first started making money I was living way outside of my means and all it took was a couple mistakes to bring me all the way down back to zero."*')
para("Jordan admits he made the exact mistake he's warning about. I call this a damaging admission: say the bad thing about yourself first, and the advice that follows becomes more believable.")
para("Then he answers the objection everyone has about a rich guy warning people not to spend money: *\"if you think that I'm a hypocrite for saying this while driving my car, living in the house, wearing expensive watches...\"* He names the objection out loud before the comments can.")
h2('The Enemy')
para('*"These car dealerships prey on uneducated people and sign them up for the most insane deals ever keeping them crippled in debt for a lifetime."*')
para("This is the enemy. Car dealers, credit cards, student loans, a broken system. It's the \"throw rocks at their enemies\" part of the Blair Warren line, and the \"justify their failures\" part too. If the viewer is in debt, the script tells them it's partly because the system is built to trap them.")
para("I talk about this a lot. When you take the blame off the viewer and put it on something outside them, they feel understood. And people stick with the creator who understands them.")
para("Now, a fair question about this step: most of these viewers have an earning problem, not a spending problem, so why is it here? Because it does a job the other steps can't. It gives the video an enemy, it's the step where Jordan admits a mistake, and it heads off the hypocrite comments. Some sections are there to build trust more than to teach.")

para("**For your business:** pick an enemy your buyer already resents, like lead-gen agencies selling junk leads, or gurus selling hacks. And admit one mistake you made in your own business. That one line will do a lot for trust.")

# ================= STEP 5 =================
h1('Step 5 And The Ending: Raise The Goal And Tie It Back')
h2('The Last Step')
para('*"The last step is to immediately set a new bigger goal for yourself for the next 30 days and once you hit it you raise the bar again."*')
para("Notice this step is short. It mostly repeats the first four steps on a loop: read, plan, collect wins, avoid traps, then raise the goal. That works as a recap without sounding like one.")
para('*"And if you think that just doing the same thing over and over again is boring, it\'s because it kind of is."*')
para("Admitting the boring part makes everything else in the video more believable.")
h2('The Belief Shift')
para('*"Making money is just doing the right set of actions over and over again for a long period of time until you become rich. There\'s no magic wand that will 100x your net worth overnight."*')
para("This is the last belief the script needs to break: that getting rich is complicated and only for geniuses. Jordan says it's a handful of skills, a strong mind, and showing up for a long time.")
h2('The Dream State')
para('*"While everyone else is watching Netflix and making excuses you\'ll be out there every single day putting in the work to transform your life."*')
para("Remember the pain state from the intro, with the scrolling and the TV and the autopilot? This is the opposite picture. The viewer gets to see themselves on the other side, and it contrasts directly with the people from the intro.")
para("That's the roller coaster again: low at the start, high at the end.")
h2('The Last Line')
para('*"...and then when you look back you\'ll realize that getting richer than 99% of people was actually pretty simple."*')
para("It closes the very first loop from the very first sentence. The title said getting rich is easy, the hook asked whether it could be easier than you think, and the last line says it was simple all along.")
para("My rule for endings: deliver the final payoff, then end fast. That's what this does. There's no \"thanks for watching\" and no long goodbye. The video ends right there.")
para("**Try this:** read the first and last line of your last script one after the other. If they don't sound like they belong together, rewrite the last line so it answers the first.")

# ================= QUESTIONS =================
h1('Questions People Ask About This Script')
para("These came up when my team reviewed this script, plus the ones business owners ask me about it.")
h2('Why call it a "strategy" and not a "5-step framework"?')
para("Because the hook is selling something easy. \"30 minutes a day\" and \"one strategy\" sound light. \"A 5-step framework\" sounds like homework before the viewer has even decided to stay. The steps get revealed one at a time, inside the video, once the viewer is already hooked.")
h2('Why does the what sometimes come before the why?')
para("In step 1, the why comes first, because \"believe you can get rich\" is the step people are most likely to dismiss. In steps 2 to 5, the open loop names the step early, because those steps are simple and concrete. Saying \"set a 30-day goal\" right away doesn't give anything away, because the value is in how you set it. Lead with the why when the step sounds obvious or easy to dismiss. Lead with the what when the step is clear and the how is the payoff.")
h2('Why "the top 1%" and not "the top 10%"?')
para("The title and the hook needed the biggest believable number. \"Richer than 99% of people\" is a bigger, cleaner promise than top 10%, and the script makes it believable by showing how low the bar is. The sales page can go narrower once someone is already interested.")
h2("Why pitch the offer so early?")
para("Because most viewers are gone by the end. The trick is to give real value first and to make the offer the answer to a problem the video just raised.")
h2('How involved was Jordan?')
para("Jordan picked the video idea and sent us videos he liked for inspiration. We wrote the outline, he signed off on it, and then we wrote the full script for the teleprompter. The stories in it are Jordan's own life, his single mom, the $30,000 of debt, and the books he actually read. We never make up a client's story. Our job is to find the right one and put it in the right place.")

h2('I sell a $5,000 service, not a $37 course. Where does my pitch go?')
para("Same spot, after your first or second step, but the ask is different. Nobody buys a $5,000 service on impulse, so pitch the next small step: book a call, grab a free resource, or apply. Mention it once in the middle as the answer to a problem, then once more at the end for the people who stayed. The ones who watched all the way through are your warmest leads.")
h2("What if I don't have a broke-to-rich story?")
para("You don't need one. Use a client's before and after with a real number, or your own small version: the month your calendar was empty, the offer that flopped. The proof line only has to answer one question: why should I listen to you?")
h2("My viewers are business owners. Won't a pain state insult them?")
para("It will if you mock them. Jordan's pain state is about \"the average person\", which works for his viewer because nobody thinks they're average. For a business owner, describe the situation, not the person: the empty calendar, the referrals that dried up, the ads that got expensive. Then blame the system, not them.")
h2('Does this work on a small channel?')
para("Yes. A big channel gave this script a big launch, but the structure is what keeps people watching, and that works at 500 subscribers too. If you sell something, 2,000 of the right viewers can be worth more than 900,000 of the wrong ones.")

# ================= YOUR BUSINESS =================
h1("Make It Work For Your Business")
para("Jordan sells a $37 program to beginners. You might sell $5,000 consulting to business owners. You'll change the words, and you can keep the structure.")
para("Here's every move in this script, and what it could look like for a sales consultant who helps agencies book more calls:")
table([['The move', 'Jordan\'s version', 'A sales consultant\'s version'],
       ['Shocking question', 'Richer than 99% with 30 minutes a day', 'What if you could book 20 calls a month without cold calling?'],
       ['Proof', 'Broke, then $10 million online', 'From 3 calls a month to 40, for my own agency (use your real numbers)'],
       ['Pain state', 'Autopilot, scrolling, no goals', 'Hoping referrals show up, chasing dead leads, an empty calendar'],
       ['The turn', 'The bar is so low', 'Most agencies never follow up more than once'],
       ['The math', '30 minutes a day is 180 hours a year', '5 follow-ups a day is 100 conversations a month'],
       ['What you don\'t have to do', 'No 5 a.m. runs, no 12-hour days', 'No cold calling, no paid ads, no new hires'],
       ['Relatability', '$30,000 in debt', 'The month I almost shut down my agency'],
       ['Offer as the answer', 'Not sure which skill? Digital Launchpad', 'Not sure what to say in your follow-ups? Book a call and we\'ll map yours out'],
       ['Enemy', 'Car dealers and credit cards', 'Lead-gen agencies that sell you junk leads'],
       ['Tie back', 'It was actually pretty simple', 'A full calendar was never about luck']], [1.5, 2.3, 2.7])
para("Two rules when you swap it over. Only use proof that's true and that you can show on screen. And keep the enemy as something outside the viewer, like a bad system or a bad industry habit, never the viewer themselves.")

# ================= TEMPLATE =================
h1('How To Write Your Script With This Skeleton (Step By Step)')
h2('Step 1: Pick One Viewer And One Offer')
para("Write one sentence: who the video is for, and what you want them to do after it. For Jordan it was \"someone who hasn't made $1,000 online yet, and I want them to join Digital Launchpad\". If you can't write that sentence, you're not ready to script.")
h2('Step 2: Write The Intro, One Job Per Line')
para("Fill in these eight lines, in this order:")
bullets(['Confirm the click with the promise from your title.',
         'Set the stakes: what they win if they listen, or lose if they don\'t.',
         'Prove it with a before and after and a real number.',
         'Tell them what they\'ll get from the video.',
         'Paint the pain state your viewer is scared of.',
         'Flip a belief they walked in with.',
         'Answer the "too good to be true" objection, with math if you can.',
         'List the hard things they won\'t have to do.'], numbered=True)
h2('Step 3: Outline 3 To 5 Steps')
para("For each step, write four things: the open loop, the why, the what and the how. Make each how something the viewer can do in the next 24 hours.")
para("Then give each step one extra move from this script: a story from your life, a mistake you made, a reference people know, a name for your idea, or an enemy.")
h2('Step 4: Place Your Offer')
para("Put it after the first or second step, as the answer to a problem that step raises. Write the line before it so it creates the need.")
h2('Step 5: Write The Ending Last')
para("Paint the dream state, the opposite of your pain state. Then write a last line that answers your first line, and stop there.")
h2('Step 6: Read It Out Loud')
para("Read the whole thing out loud once. Anywhere you stumble, the sentence is too long. Anywhere you get bored, the viewer will too. Cut it.")

# ================= CHECKLIST =================
h1('Your Script Checklist')
h2('The Quick Version')
para("Run every script through this before you film:")
checklist(['My first line says what my title promised',
           'I give proof with a real number in the first 30 seconds',
           'Every line in my intro has one job I can name',
           'I show the pain state before the solution',
           'I answer the biggest objection before the viewer can ask it',
           'Every step has an open loop, a why, a what and a how',
           'Every how can be done in the next 24 hours',
           'My offer comes before most viewers drop off, as the answer to a problem',
           'There\'s at least one story and one mistake that are really mine',
           'My last line answers my first line'])

h2('The Full Checklist My Writers Use')
para("This is the exact quality checklist every Viral Bangers scriptwriter runs before a script goes to a client. If you write your own scripts, you're the client, so read \"client\" as \"you\".")
para("**Phase 1: Before you write.** Do this before your first draft.")
para("*Client and audience*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Target audience defined: who exactly is watching? (Age, interests, how much they already know.) Make sure you understand what drives them.",
           "Client voice and tone set: how should this sound? Casual, authoritative, funny?",
           "Video goal clear: what should viewers do, feel or learn after watching?",
           "Client-specific requirements noted: any formatting, CTAs or style preferences?"])
para("*Content strategy*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Hook strategy planned: which hook type fits this topic and audience?",
           "Key message identified: what's the ONE big idea the viewer takes away?",
           "Supporting points: 2 to 4 main points that back up the key message, so the script doesn't wander off it.",
           "Call to action decided: what specific action should viewers take?"])
para("**Phase 2: Hook and structure.** Do this after writing the hook and the outline.")
para("*The hook*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Click confirmed: the first 3 lines match the title and thumbnail.",
           "Curiosity gap: the hook asks a question or creates intrigue without giving everything away.",
           "Stakes set: it's clear why the viewer should care and what they'll get.",
           "Objections handled: the viewer's biggest doubts are answered up front.",
           "Simple language: no jargon, complex terms or insider language.",
           "Length: the hook is under 30 seconds when read out loud."])
para("*The structure*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Logical flow: each section builds naturally into the next.",
           "Open loops: curiosity keeps going through the whole video, not just the hook.",
           "Retention: pattern interrupts, transitions and mini-hooks throughout.",
           "Value spread out: the best insights are spread through the video, not all at the start."])
para("**Phase 3: Content quality.** Do this after the full first draft.")
para("*Accuracy and research*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Facts checked: every statistic, claim and number is accurate.",
           "Sources known: you know where each claim comes from, even if it isn't in the script. Check for anything AI made up.",
           "Examples relevant: every story and example supports a main point.",
           "No contradictions: the script doesn't contradict itself or known facts."])
para("*Language and readability*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Reading level matches how much your audience already knows.",
           "Conversational: it sounds natural when read out loud.",
           "Active voice: stronger and more engaging than passive voice.",
           "Sentence variety: a mix of short punchy sentences and longer ones that explain.",
           "Fluff cut: no \"basically\", \"actually\" or extra qualifiers."])
para("*Value and engagement*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Actionable: viewers can use what they learn.",
           "Specific examples: concrete details, not vague generalisations.",
           "Emotional connection: it speaks to what viewers have lived through and what they want.",
           "Unique angle: a fresh take, not the same advice everyone else gives."])
para("**Phase 4: Technical script elements.** Format and technical review.")
para("*Visual and audio cues*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Visual directions: clear notes for the editor on B-roll, graphics and text on screen.",
           "Transitions marked: the bridges between sections are clear.",
           "Emphasis marked: important words and phrases flagged for vocal emphasis.",
           "Pacing marked: pauses, speed changes and tone shifts noted."])
para("*Call to action*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Clear and specific: viewers know exactly what to do next.",
           "Motivated: there's a clear reason to take the action.",
           "Placed well: it comes when the viewer is most engaged.",
           "One main CTA: not several asks competing with each other."])
para("**Phase 5: Final polish.** Before it goes to the client.")
para("*Flow test*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Read-aloud test: the script flows naturally when spoken.",
           "Timing: the estimated length matches the target video length.",
           "Retention: engagement stays up the whole way, with no dead spots.",
           "Smooth transitions: no jarring jumps between topics.",
           "AI writing check: none of the patterns in the list below."])
para("*The AI writing check*", blank_after=False)
para("If a script has these, it reads like a robot wrote it. Cut them:")
bullets(["Magic adverbs like \"quietly\", \"deeply\", \"fundamentally\" or \"remarkably\".",
         "AI words like \"delve\", \"leverage\", \"utilize\", \"robust\", \"streamline\" and \"harness\".",
         "Fancy nouns like \"tapestry\", \"landscape\", \"paradigm\", \"synergy\" and \"ecosystem\".",
         "\"Serves as\" or \"stands as\" where \"is\" would do.",
         "\"It's not X, it's Y\" reframes. One in a script is fine. Five is a problem.",
         "\"Not X. Not Y. Just Z.\" countdowns.",
         "Asking a question nobody asked and answering it straight away, like \"The result? Devastating.\"",
         "Starting sentence after sentence with the same words.",
         "Lists of three, back to back to back.",
         "Filler like \"It's worth noting\", \"Interestingly\" and \"Importantly\".",
         "Endings like \"highlighting its importance\" that add fake meaning.",
         "\"From X to Y\" when X and Y aren't on any real scale.",
         "Every sentence chopped into its own dramatic one-line paragraph.",
         "Fake suspense like \"Here's the kicker\" or \"Here's the thing\".",
         "\"Think of it as...\" analogies that are less clear than the point.",
         "\"Imagine a world where...\"",
         "Fake honesty, like \"And yes, I'll admit it...\"",
         "Saying something is obvious or simple instead of showing it.",
         "Making every point sound world-changing.",
         "\"Let's break this down\", \"Let's unpack this\" or \"Let's dive in\".",
         "\"Experts say\" with no name. If you can't name the source, you don't have one.",
         "Made-up labels like \"the acceleration trap\" used as if they're real terms.",
         "Em dashes, arrows and curly quotes.",
         "Bullet points that all start with a bold phrase.",
         "Summarising every section, or using the same metaphor over and over."])
para("(The AI writing list is adapted from [[tropes.fyi|https://tropes.fyi]] by Ossama.)")
para("*Client standards*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Formatting matches the client's preferred style.",
           "Grammar and spelling are perfect: no typos or punctuation errors.",
           "Brand voice: it sounds like the client the whole way through.",
           "Every client requirement is covered."])
para("*Final check*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Hook test: would you click away in the first 15 seconds?",
           "Value test: would you feel satisfied watching this?",
           "Share test: would you send this video to a friend?",
           "Client test: does this represent the client's brand well?"])
h2('Red Flags: Fix These Before Anything Else')
para("If any of these are true, revise the script straight away.")
para("*Content*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Clickbait: the title promises something the video doesn't deliver.",
           "Boring opening: the first 30 seconds are slow, confusing or generic.",
           "Information dump: long stretches with no story or engagement.",
           "Weak ending: the video fizzles out, or ends without a clear next step."])
para("*Technical*", blank_after=False)
checklist(["Too complex: jargon or ideas too advanced for the audience.",
           "Wrong length: well over or under the target length.",
           "Missing CTA: no clear direction for what to do next.",
           "Voice mismatch: it doesn't sound like the client."])
h2('Adjust It For Your Channel')
para("Different channels and videos need different things. Put the extra weight here:")
table([['If it\'s...', 'Focus extra on'],
       ['An educational channel', 'Accuracy, simplicity and steps people can use'],
       ['An entertainment channel', 'Hooks, energy and shareability'],
       ['A business or authority channel', 'Credibility, professionalism and a clear value proposition'],
       ['A faceless channel', 'Strong visual cues and a clear narration flow'],
       ['A how-to video', 'Step-by-step clarity and practical value'],
       ['A story or case study', 'The story arc, emotional connection and relatability'],
       ['A list video', 'Smooth transitions, varied pacing and a strong opening']], [2.6, 3.9])
para("Then add 3 to 5 requirements that are specific to you or your client:")
checklist(["Requirement 1", "Requirement 2", "Requirement 3", "Requirement 4", "Requirement 5"])

